WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:05.840
Australia has committed to 82 percent renewable electricity by 2030.

00:06.290 --> 00:07.018
Is it on track?

00:09.400 --> 00:17.293
In 2005, about 9 percent of Australia's electricity was renewable. Coal and gas made the rest.

00:18.693 --> 00:24.531
Then the share stalled at 13 to 15 percent while the Renewable Energy Target was under review.

00:24.981 --> 00:36.457
After the 2015 reset, it grew about 2.5 points a year, nearly five times its 2005 to 2015 pace, reaching 35 percent in 2024.

00:36.907 --> 00:42.334
Fossil generation fell from 208 to 182 terawatt hours.

00:43.834 --> 00:54.235
Solar and wind took over. They made 4.7 percent of renewable electricity in 2005, and 81.3 percent in 2024.

00:54.685 --> 01:00.086
Wind overtook hydro in 2019. Solar overtook both a year later.

01:01.586 --> 01:10.505
By volume, China leads, with 3,399 terawatt hours of renewable electricity, against Australia's 98.

01:10.955 --> 01:19.000
By share, the story flips. New Zealand leads at 85.5 percent, then Germany at 54.5.

01:19.450 --> 01:24.362
Australia, at 35, just edges China at 33.7.

01:25.962 --> 01:33.385
On a linear trend, the share reaches 37.8 percent by 2030, less than half the 82 percent target.

01:33.835 --> 01:37.268
By 2035 it reaches 44.6 percent.

01:38.868 --> 01:42.486
Australia is moving in the right direction, but not fast enough.

01:42.936 --> 01:44.281
